rts2-base (0.1.0-5) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * debian/rules: add teld/gemini-udp/rts2-teld-gemini-udp to
    RTS2_BASE_BINARIES. The driver has built since it was added but was
    never listed here, so it never reached an installed system - and
    because /etc/rts2/devices addresses drivers as rts2-$device-$type,
    the only symptom was a telescope that silently failed to start.
  * kernel/daemon: daemonize handshake. doDaemonize() forked and the
    parent exited 0 before lockFile(), bind(), initValues() and the
    driver's initHardware() had run, with the child's stdout/stderr
    already on /dev/null - so a daemon that died during initialisation
    still reported a successful start, and left no trace outside syslog.
    The forking parent now waits on a pipe until the child says
    initialisation finished, and the child keeps the starting terminal
    until then. Fork placement is unchanged deliberately: drivers on
    libusb (gxccd, fli) create threads inside initHardware(), so forking
    after it would leave the daemon single-threaded with mutexes held by
    threads that no longer exist.
  * kernel/daemon: exit 255 used to mean both "lock file is held by
    another process" and "lock file could not be opened at all", which
    rts2-start reported alike as "already running" - so a non-root
    invocation, or a --lock-prefix under a directory a reboot had wiped,
    claimed a running system where there was none. The two are now 255
    and 254 (RTS2_EXIT_ALREADY_RUNNING / RTS2_EXIT_LOCK_ERROR), and a
    missing lock directory is created rather than being an error.
  * packaging: rts2-start rewritten; rts2-stop is now a front end for it
    rather than a second, drifting copy of the same logic. It reports
    what actually happened rather than what fork() returned, shows a
    failed daemon's output instead of discarding it, finds daemons on
    the PATH (the rtspy services install to /usr/local/bin and so could
    never be started), honours a per-daemon --lock-prefix when locating
    lock files, decides "is it running" from the lock's flock rather
    than from a pid that can go stale and be recycled, refuses to kill a
    pid that is not the expected program, waits for a daemon to actually
    stop before restarting it, and grows --status.

 -- RTS2 Project <petr@rts2.org>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 17:38:00 +0200

rts2-base (0.1.0-4) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * debian/control, debian/rules: split rts2-camd-gxccd out of rts2-base
    into a new binary package, rts2-drivers-gxccd, same pattern as the
    earlier rts2-drivers-fli split (same source, same build, no extra
    compilation; empty on a host without BASE_GXCCD_SDK_DIR set).
  * debian/rules: BASE_GXCCD_SDK_DIR no longer has a hardcoded default
    (was a stale personal path) - single-sourced from the environment
    the same way BASE_FLI_SDK_DIR already is, so buildme.sh and this
    packaging build can't disagree on the path.
  * rts2-teld-paramount (libmks3) was considered for the same split but
    deliberately left out - the informal non-redistribution
    understanding around libmks3 is about whether to publish it at all,
    not a technical question, and that's not this package's call to make.

 -- RTS2 Project <petr@rts2.org>  Mon, 20 Jul 2026 23:58:00 +0200

rts2-base (0.1.0-3) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * debian/control, debian/rules: split the FLI (Finger Lakes
    Instrumentation) drivers out of rts2-base into a new binary package,
    rts2-drivers-fli (rts2-camd-fli, rts2-focusd-fli, rts2-filterd-fli).
    Built from the same source/same dpkg-buildpackage run - no extra
    compilation - but keeps rts2-base itself buildable on a host without
    the (non-redistributed) vendor libfli SDK; on such a host
    rts2-drivers-fli just comes out empty rather than failing the build.
    gxccd stays in rts2-base for now (not asked to split, and unlike FLI
    it's a single driver with no separate focuser/filter siblings).

 -- RTS2 Project <petr@rts2.org>  Mon, 20 Jul 2026 23:35:00 +0200

rts2-base (0.1.0-2) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * packaging/rts2.ini: set [observatory] base_path explicitly to
    /var/lib/rts2/images/ (rts2-db's postinst creates and owns this
    directory). The code default of "/images/" when unset is root-owned
    and unwritable by the "rts2" user rts2-executor runs as - found via a
    live install where image writes were silently failing (no error
    logged - see the matching fitsfile.cpp fix). Only affects fresh
    installs of this conffile; existing customized rts2.ini files are
    untouched by design (standard conffile handling) and need the line
    added by hand.
  * kernel/src/fitsfile.cpp: FitsFile::createImage() logged an error on
    every failure path except mkpath() failing - added the missing
    logStream() call so a bad image base path fails loudly instead of
    silently.

 -- RTS2 Project <petr@rts2.org>  Mon, 20 Jul 2026 00:05:00 +0200

rts2-base (0.1.0-1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Initial rts2-base package: core (centrald, wire protocol, rts2-mon)
    plus every statically-linkable driver family from the base CMake
    port (dummy, D50/SBT/FRAM mounts and domes, focusers, filter wheels,
    sensors, FLI and gxccd cameras). Excludes Andor (vendor SDK ships
    only a shared library, no static option) and Paramount (libmks3
    source is under an informal non-redistribution understanding, not
    vendored into this build).

